// Runtime/CommandLineRuntime.maxon — argv, split out of the one string Windows gives a process.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT IT SERVES.** `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`'s `args()` bottoms out in two `__Builtins` intrinsics —
// `commandLineCount()` and `commandLineArg(i)` — and nothing else in the language reaches these entry
// points. The bootstrap declares the same two (`2-Parser.cs:10952-10957`) with the same arity, the same
// types and the same ownership, so the SURFACE here is the reference's; only the implementation differs.
//
// ⭐⭐ **TWO LANES AS OF MAC7, AND THEY RUN DIFFERENT Std GRAPHS — the only family in this compiler that
// does.** This paragraph used to read "HOST-ONLY BY THE R4 RULING (2026-07-30), AND FOR A REASON OF ITS OWN
// ON TOP", and that reason of its own is exactly why the fork exists rather than a lowering. A target that
// provides neither still refuses at the call site with the EXISTING `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct`,
// which matches on the `__cl_` PREFIX — so an entry point added later is gated by construction rather than
// by memory.
//
// ⭐⭐ **arm64-macOS SERVES IT AS OF MAC7, AND THE PARAGRAPH BELOW IS WHY THAT TOOK A RUNG RATHER THAN A
// LOWERING.** It ends *"serving them honestly means a per-target capture plus a target-neutral surface over
// it — a rung, not a lowering"*, and that is exactly what landed: the ENTRY STUB captures `argc`/`argv`
// into two `.data` words, and this file builds ONE of TWO Std graphs over them depending on which shape the
// target's process start-up has. Neither graph contains a target test; `commandLineShape` asks once, at
// install.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE EXTRA REASON MATTERS, because it is not "nobody wrote the lowering yet": THE SHAPE OF argv IS
// NOT THE SAME ON THE OTHER PLATFORMS.** Windows hands a process ONE UNSPLIT STRING and the splitting is
// code (this file). SysV hands `_start` a PRE-SPLIT VECTOR — argc at `[rsp]`, argv at `rsp+8` on x64-linux
// (`Targets/BackendDispatch.maxon:413`), argc/argv in x0/x1 on arm64-macos (`Arm64Runtime.maxon:120-121`) —
// where there is nothing to split and the work is instead to capture two registers at entry and keep them.
// Those are two different mechanisms behind one surface, and shv2's Std tier is TARGET-NEUTRAL: there is no
// place in it for a body that is a splitter on one target and an indexed load on another. Serving them
// honestly means a per-target capture plus a target-neutral surface over it — a rung, not a lowering — so
// this rung builds the Windows half and REFUSES the rest LOUDLY, which is the one thing that must not be
// traded for a target that compiles and answers an empty argv.
// ⚠ wasm is refused for a second, independent reason: WASI does have `args_sizes_get`/`args_get`, but the
// wasm lane is SCALAR (no heap, no `String`, no `print`) and `args()` answers an `Array with String`. A
// lowering there would be vocabulary with no reachable consumer.
//
// ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SPLIT IS THE CRT's, NOT A SIMPLER ONE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** `GetCommandLineA` yields the
// raw line; every argument boundary, every quote and every backslash run then has to be read the way the
// rest of the platform reads them, because the SHELL and the SPAWNING PROCESS both encoded the arguments
// under those rules. A whitespace-only split would be a silent wrong answer for `--note="a b"` — the exact
// defect class this project hunts — and it would DISAGREE WITH THE BOOTSTRAP, which gets the real rules for
// free from shell32's `CommandLineToArgvW`. The rules, from that function's contract:
//
//   • space and tab separate arguments, unless inside quotes;
//   • `2n` backslashes then `"` ⇒ `n` backslashes, and the `"` toggles quoting;
//   • `2n+1` backslashes then `"` ⇒ `n` backslashes and a LITERAL `"`;
//   • backslashes not followed by `"` ⇒ themselves, all of them;
//   • `""` inside quotes ⇒ a literal `"`, still quoted.
//
// ⚠ **argv[0] IS PARSED BY THE SAME RULES, WHERE `CommandLineToArgvW` GIVES IT ITS OWN.** The real rule for
// argv[0] is "quoted ⇒ up to the next `\"`; otherwise up to the first whitespace", with NO backslash
// processing. The two agree on every program path that can actually occur: an unquoted `C:\dir\prog.exe`
// has backslashes none of which precede a `"`, so the general rule emits them all unchanged, and a quoted
// `"C:\dir\prog.exe"` opens and closes on its two quotes with the same result. They part company only for a
// path ending in a backslash INSIDE quotes (`"C:\dir\"`), which no executable path is. Written down rather
// than discovered: a second scanner for one special case would be a second set of rules to keep in step,
// for an input that does not arise.
//
// ⚠ **ENCODING: ANSI, NOT UTF-8, AND ON PURPOSE** — see `IatSlot.GetCommandLineA` for the whole argument.
// The short form: shv2's Windows lane is `A`-entry-point end to end, so argv must be too, or the first
// `openRead(args.get(1))` misreads its own path.

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SHAPES A PLATFORM CAN HAND A PROCESS ITS ARGUMENTS IN — the one fact this family forks on.**
// It is asked ONCE, at install, and each answer builds a complete Std graph; nothing downstream of that ever
// asks which target it is compiling for, which is what keeps the ops target-neutral while the SURFACE stays
// one surface.
//
// ⚠ It is keyed on the OS and not on the CPU, because it is an ABI fact: every SysV platform hands `_start`
// a pre-split vector and Windows hands `GetCommandLineA` one string, whatever the ISA.
enum CommandLineShape
	// Windows: ONE unsplit string, split here by the CRT's rules (see the scanner below).
	unsplitString
	// SysV: a pre-split vector the process start-up already holds. `argc` and `argv` reach the Std tier
	// through the two `.data` words below, which the target's entry stub fills before `main` runs.
	argumentVector
end 'CommandLineShape'

// ⚠ **WASI ANSWERS `argumentVector` AND THAT IS A SHAPE ANSWER, NOT A CAPABILITY CLAIM.**
// `args_sizes_get`/`args_get` hand a component a pre-split vector exactly as SysV hands a process one, so
// the SHAPE really is that one — what that lane lacks is the entry-stub capture and a reachable consumer
// (`args()` answers an `Array with String`), and `targetProvidesFacility` is where that refusal lives. A
// shape function that lied about the shape to express a capability would put the same fact in two places.
function commandLineShape(target Target) returns CommandLineShape
	return match target.os 'os'
		windows gives CommandLineShape.unsplitString
		linux to wasi gives CommandLineShape.argumentVector
	end 'os'
end 'commandLineShape'

// ⭐ **THE THREE `.data` WORDS THE VECTOR SHAPE READS.** Two are the captured vector; the third is the
// answer an out-of-range index gets.
//
// ⚠ **`__cl_empty_arg` IS A WORD OF ZEROES USED AS A C STRING, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A GLOBAL RATHER THAN
// AN ALLOCATION.** `__cl_arg` answers the empty string for an index outside the vector (the bootstrap's
// documented answer), and every OTHER exit hands `emitCStringToManaged` a pointer it does not own — so an
// allocated empty string would be the ONE exit with a release to remember, on the coldest path in the file.
// A `.data` word is zero by construction and is never written.
//
// ⚠ **THEY ARE SEALED BEFORE ANY WORKER M EXISTS** — the entry stub writes them before `main`, and nothing
// writes them again — which is `MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers` exactly.
export let CommandLineArgcLabel = b"__cl_argc"
export let CommandLineArgvLabel = b"__cl_argv"
let CommandLineEmptyArgLabel = b"__cl_empty_arg"

// The `.data` slots this family lays out — nothing at all under the Windows shape, where the command line
// is fetched by a call rather than captured at entry.
export function commandLineRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if not usage.usesCommandLine 'noCommandLine'
		return out
	end 'noCommandLine'

	match commandLineShape(target) 'shape'
		unsplitString then break 'shape'
		argumentVector then pushCapturedArgvGlobals(out)
	end 'shape'

	return out
end 'commandLineRuntimeGlobals'

// The three words the captured shape reads. Its own function because a match arm carries one statement, and
// because the three belong together: two are the capture and the third is what an index outside it answers.
function pushCapturedArgvGlobals(out DataSectionEntryArray)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: CommandLineArgcLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: CommandLineArgvLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: CommandLineEmptyArgLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
end 'pushCapturedArgvGlobals'

// The `__cl_` prefix that identifies EVERY command-line runtime entry point — the one test
// `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set `usesCommandLine`, and the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104.
let CommandLineRuntimePrefix = b"__cl_"

// ⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS.** Two answer an intrinsic; `__cl_scan_one` is the shared scanner both walk
// with, and it is a real runtime function rather than an inlined builder because BOTH walkers need the
// identical loop and a builder would put two copies of the CRT rules in the emitted image.
export let CommandLineCountName = b"__cl_count"
export let CommandLineArgName = b"__cl_arg"
let CommandLineScanOneName = b"__cl_scan_one"

// `__cl_scan_one`'s "there is no argument here" answer, and a NEGATIVE position rather than a flag: a byte
// position is non-negative by construction, so -1 is outside the answer domain rather than inside it, and
// both callers test `>= 0`. (`__str_find`'s `StrFindNotFound` was the other constant carrying this
// argument; it retired with the P1.8 Slice C searches at W49 wave 5, which is why the reasoning is spelled
// out here rather than cross-referenced.)
let NoMoreArguments = -1 as ParsedInt

// The two bytes that separate arguments outside quotes, and the quote and escape that do the rest. Named
// because a bare `0x20` at a branch says nothing about which of the two roles a byte is being tested for.
let SpaceByte = 0x20 as ParsedInt
let TabByte = 0x09 as ParsedInt
let QuoteByte = 0x22 as ParsedInt
let BackslashByte = 0x5C as ParsedInt

// The scanner's quoting state, as the 0/1 it rides the loop as. `not q` is spelled `1 - q` at the one site
// that toggles it, which is why both values are named rather than just the true one.
let OutsideQuotes = 0 as ParsedInt
let InsideQuotes = 1 as ParsedInt

// The scratch buffer holds one argument plus its NUL. An unescaped argument is never LONGER than the
// command line it came from — every rule above consumes at least as many bytes as it emits — so the whole
// line's length plus this one byte is a sufficient bound, and the only one that needs no second scan. The
// bound is TIGHT: the terminator is written at the write cursor, which reaches `length` in the worst case.
//
// ⚠ It is a COUNT OF BYTES, not a byte VALUE, and the name says so because `NulByte` (the value 0) is in
// scope in this same file. Spelling this one `NulTerminatorByte` invited exactly one edit — using `NulByte`
// here — and that edit sizes the scratch at `length` and overflows it by one on the terminating store.
let NulTerminatorSlackBytes = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **`__cl_scan_one(cmd, pos, dest) -> nextPos`** — skip separators, then copy ONE unescaped argument into
// `dest` NUL-terminated and answer the position just past it, or `NoMoreArguments` when the line is spent.
//
// ⭐ **IT WRITES A cstring BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT ITS CALLER ALREADY KNOWS HOW TO CONSUME.** `__cl_arg` turns
// the scratch into an owned `__ManagedMemory` through `emitCStringToManaged`, the copy `__md_filename` and
// `__md_current_path` already go through; answering with a length instead would have meant a second return
// value, and a second return value at this tier is a heap-boxed pair (`emitCStringToManaged`'s own note).
//
// ⚠ **THE BACKSLASH RUN IS COUNTED, NOT PROCESSED PAIRWISE, AND THE PAIRWISE FORM IS WRONG.** It is
// tempting to consume `\\` as one emitted backslash and be done; MEASURED against the rules above, that
// answers `a\b` for the input `a\\b`, because outside a quote-terminated run every backslash stands for
// ITSELF. The count is what tells the two contexts apart, so the scanner counts first and decides after.
//
// The three exits of the run — plain, even-before-quote, odd-before-quote — differ only in how many
// backslashes to emit, where to resume, and whether a literal `"` follows. So they compute those THREE
// NUMBERS and share ONE emit loop, rather than each carrying a copy of it.
function buildCommandLineScanOne(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CommandLineScanOneName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var skipHead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clskip")
	var skipTabTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clskiptab")
	var skipStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clskipstep")
	var argStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clstart")
	var noMore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clnone")
	var scan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clscan")
	var notNul = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clnotnul")
	var notBackslash = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clnotbs")
	var maybeSeparator = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clmaysep")
	var sepSpaceTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clsepsp")
	var sepTabTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clseptab")
	var copyByte = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clcopy")
	var atQuote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clquote")
	var quoteInside = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clqin")
	var literalQuote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clqlit")
	var toggleQuotes = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clqtog")
	var countRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbscnt")
	var countStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbsstep")
	var runEnded = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbsend")
	var runPlain = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbspln")
	var runBeforeQuote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbsq")
	var emitRun = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbsemit")
	var emitRunStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbswr")
	var runTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbstail")
	var runTailQuote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clbstq")
	var terminate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clterm")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let cmd = 0 as ValueId
	let pos = 1 as ValueId
	let dest = 2 as ValueId

	// --- skip the separators before the argument ---
	let skipAt = ids.mint()
	skipHead.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(skipAt, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(skipHead.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(skipHead.id, argIds: argsOf(pos)))

	// ⚠ `skipTabTest`, `skipStep` and `argStart` carry NO block args and take no edge arguments: `skipHead`
	// dominates all three (it is the only way into the first, which is the only way into the other two), so
	// `skipAt` is simply in scope — the dominance argument `emitCStringLength` states for its own length.
	emitSeparatorTest(module, block: skipHead, cmd: cmd, at: skipAt, byte: SpaceByte, hit: skipStep, miss: skipTabTest, ids: ids)
	emitSeparatorTest(module, block: skipTabTest, cmd: cmd, at: skipAt, byte: TabByte, hit: skipStep, miss: argStart, ids: ids)

	let oneStep = emitConst(module, block: skipStep, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let skipNext = emitBin(module, block: skipStep, a: skipAt, b: oneStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(skipStep, op: StdOp.branch(skipHead.id))
	skipStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(skipHead.id, argIds: argsOf(skipNext)))

	// --- is there an argument at all? ---
	let startByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: argStart, base: cmd, index: skipAt, ids: ids)
	let startNul = emitConst(module, block: argStart, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	let lineSpent = emitCmp(module, block: argStart, a: startByte, b: startNul, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(argStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(lineSpent, thenBlockId: noMore.id, elseBlockId: scan.id))
	let zeroWrite = emitConst(module, block: argStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let outside = emitConst(module, block: argStart, value: OutsideQuotes, ids: ids)
	argStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: threeArgs(skipAt, second: zeroWrite, third: outside)))

	let none = emitConst(module, block: noMore, value: NoMoreArguments, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noMore, op: StdOp.ret(none))

	// --- the scan loop: (read cursor, write cursor, quoting state) ---
	let at = ids.mint()
	let wrote = ids.mint()
	let quoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(scan, values: threeArgs(at, second: wrote, third: quoted))

	let cur = emitLoadByte(module, block: scan, base: cmd, index: at, ids: ids)
	let nul = emitConst(module, block: scan, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	let lineEnded = emitCmp(module, block: scan, a: cur, b: nul, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scan, op: StdOp.condBranch(lineEnded, thenBlockId: terminate.id, elseBlockId: notNul.id))
	scan.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(terminate.id, argIds: twoArgs(at, second: wrote)))
	scan.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(notNul.id, argIds: threeArgs(at, second: wrote, third: quoted)))

	let notNulAt = ids.mint()
	let notNulWrote = ids.mint()
	let notNulQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(notNul, values: threeArgs(notNulAt, second: notNulWrote, third: notNulQuoted))
	let notNulByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: notNul, base: cmd, index: notNulAt, ids: ids)
	let bsConst = emitConst(module, block: notNul, value: BackslashByte, ids: ids)
	let isBackslash = emitCmp(module, block: notNul, a: notNulByte, b: bsConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notNul, op: StdOp.condBranch(isBackslash, thenBlockId: countRun.id, elseBlockId: notBackslash.id))
	let zeroRun = emitConst(module, block: notNul, value: 0, ids: ids)
	notNul.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countRun.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(notNulAt, second: notNulWrote, third: notNulQuoted, fourth: zeroRun)))
	notNul.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(notBackslash.id, argIds: threeArgs(notNulAt, second: notNulWrote, third: notNulQuoted)))

	let plainAt = ids.mint()
	let plainWrote = ids.mint()
	let plainQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(notBackslash, values: threeArgs(plainAt, second: plainWrote, third: plainQuoted))
	let plainByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: notBackslash, base: cmd, index: plainAt, ids: ids)
	let quoteConst = emitConst(module, block: notBackslash, value: QuoteByte, ids: ids)
	let isQuote = emitCmp(module, block: notBackslash, a: plainByte, b: quoteConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notBackslash, op: StdOp.condBranch(isQuote, thenBlockId: atQuote.id, elseBlockId: maybeSeparator.id))
	notBackslash.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(atQuote.id, argIds: threeArgs(plainAt, second: plainWrote, third: plainQuoted)))
	notBackslash.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(maybeSeparator.id, argIds: threeArgs(plainAt, second: plainWrote, third: plainQuoted)))

	// An ordinary byte: a separator only when we are OUTSIDE quotes.
	let sepAt = ids.mint()
	let sepWrote = ids.mint()
	let sepQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(maybeSeparator, values: threeArgs(sepAt, second: sepWrote, third: sepQuoted))
	let outsideConst = emitConst(module, block: maybeSeparator, value: OutsideQuotes, ids: ids)
	let isOutside = emitCmp(module, block: maybeSeparator, a: sepQuoted, b: outsideConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(maybeSeparator, op: StdOp.condBranch(isOutside, thenBlockId: sepSpaceTest.id, elseBlockId: copyByte.id))
	maybeSeparator.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sepSpaceTest.id, argIds: threeArgs(sepAt, second: sepWrote, third: sepQuoted)))
	maybeSeparator.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyByte.id, argIds: threeArgs(sepAt, second: sepWrote, third: sepQuoted)))

	emitScanSeparatorExit(module, block: sepSpaceTest, cmd: cmd, byte: SpaceByte, ends: terminate, continues: sepTabTest, ids: ids)
	emitScanSeparatorExit(module, block: sepTabTest, cmd: cmd, byte: TabByte, ends: terminate, continues: copyByte, ids: ids)

	// Copy one byte through unchanged and step both cursors.
	let copyAt = ids.mint()
	let copyWrote = ids.mint()
	let copyQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(copyByte, values: threeArgs(copyAt, second: copyWrote, third: copyQuoted))
	let copied = emitLoadByte(module, block: copyByte, base: cmd, index: copyAt, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: copyByte, base: dest, index: copyWrote, value: copied, ids: ids)
	emitResumeScan(module, block: copyByte, scan: scan, at: copyAt, atDelta: 1, wrote: copyWrote, wroteDelta: 1, quoted: copyQuoted, ids: ids)

	// A `"` with no backslashes in front of it: inside quotes a doubled `""` is a literal quote; otherwise
	// the quote toggles the state and is not itself copied.
	let qAt = ids.mint()
	let qWrote = ids.mint()
	let qQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(atQuote, values: threeArgs(qAt, second: qWrote, third: qQuoted))
	let insideConst = emitConst(module, block: atQuote, value: InsideQuotes, ids: ids)
	let isInside = emitCmp(module, block: atQuote, a: qQuoted, b: insideConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(atQuote, op: StdOp.condBranch(isInside, thenBlockId: quoteInside.id, elseBlockId: toggleQuotes.id))
	atQuote.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(quoteInside.id, argIds: threeArgs(qAt, second: qWrote, third: qQuoted)))
	atQuote.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(toggleQuotes.id, argIds: threeArgs(qAt, second: qWrote, third: qQuoted)))

	let qiAt = ids.mint()
	let qiWrote = ids.mint()
	let qiQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(quoteInside, values: threeArgs(qiAt, second: qiWrote, third: qiQuoted))
	let oneAhead = emitConst(module, block: quoteInside, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let aheadIndex = emitBin(module, block: quoteInside, a: qiAt, b: oneAhead, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let aheadByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: quoteInside, base: cmd, index: aheadIndex, ids: ids)
	let aheadQuote = emitConst(module, block: quoteInside, value: QuoteByte, ids: ids)
	let isDoubled = emitCmp(module, block: quoteInside, a: aheadByte, b: aheadQuote, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(quoteInside, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDoubled, thenBlockId: literalQuote.id, elseBlockId: toggleQuotes.id))
	quoteInside.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(literalQuote.id, argIds: threeArgs(qiAt, second: qiWrote, third: qiQuoted)))
	quoteInside.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(toggleQuotes.id, argIds: threeArgs(qiAt, second: qiWrote, third: qiQuoted)))

	let lqAt = ids.mint()
	let lqWrote = ids.mint()
	let lqQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(literalQuote, values: threeArgs(lqAt, second: lqWrote, third: lqQuoted))
	let litByte = emitConst(module, block: literalQuote, value: QuoteByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: literalQuote, base: dest, index: lqWrote, value: litByte, ids: ids)
	// BOTH quotes are consumed — that is what makes `""` one literal rather than two toggles.
	emitResumeScan(module, block: literalQuote, scan: scan, at: lqAt, atDelta: 2, wrote: lqWrote, wroteDelta: 1, quoted: lqQuoted, ids: ids)

	let tgAt = ids.mint()
	let tgWrote = ids.mint()
	let tgQuoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(toggleQuotes, values: threeArgs(tgAt, second: tgWrote, third: tgQuoted))
	let insideVal = emitConst(module, block: toggleQuotes, value: InsideQuotes, ids: ids)
	let flipped = emitBin(module, block: toggleQuotes, a: insideVal, b: tgQuoted, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let tgOne = emitConst(module, block: toggleQuotes, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let tgNext = emitBin(module, block: toggleQuotes, a: tgAt, b: tgOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(toggleQuotes, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))
	toggleQuotes.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: threeArgs(tgNext, second: tgWrote, third: flipped)))

	// --- the backslash run: count it, then decide ---
	let runAt = ids.mint()
	let runWrote = ids.mint()
	let runQuoted = ids.mint()
	let runCount = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(countRun, values: quadArgsOf(runAt, second: runWrote, third: runQuoted, fourth: runCount))
	let runByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: countRun, base: cmd, index: runAt, ids: ids)
	let runBsConst = emitConst(module, block: countRun, value: BackslashByte, ids: ids)
	let stillBackslash = emitCmp(module, block: countRun, a: runByte, b: runBsConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(countRun, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillBackslash, thenBlockId: countStep.id, elseBlockId: runEnded.id))
	countRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countStep.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(runAt, second: runWrote, third: runQuoted, fourth: runCount)))
	countRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(runEnded.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(runAt, second: runWrote, third: runQuoted, fourth: runCount)))

	let stepAt = ids.mint()
	let stepWrote = ids.mint()
	let stepQuoted = ids.mint()
	let stepCount = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(countStep, values: quadArgsOf(stepAt, second: stepWrote, third: stepQuoted, fourth: stepCount))
	let stepOne = emitConst(module, block: countStep, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let stepNextAt = emitBin(module, block: countStep, a: stepAt, b: stepOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let stepNextCount = emitBin(module, block: countStep, a: stepCount, b: stepOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(countStep, op: StdOp.branch(countRun.id))
	countStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(countRun.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(stepNextAt, second: stepWrote, third: stepQuoted, fourth: stepNextCount)))

	let endAt = ids.mint()
	let endWrote = ids.mint()
	let endQuoted = ids.mint()
	let endCount = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(runEnded, values: quadArgsOf(endAt, second: endWrote, third: endQuoted, fourth: endCount))
	let endByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: runEnded, base: cmd, index: endAt, ids: ids)
	let endQuoteConst = emitConst(module, block: runEnded, value: QuoteByte, ids: ids)
	let runHitQuote = emitCmp(module, block: runEnded, a: endByte, b: endQuoteConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(runEnded, op: StdOp.condBranch(runHitQuote, thenBlockId: runBeforeQuote.id, elseBlockId: runPlain.id))
	runEnded.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(runBeforeQuote.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(endAt, second: endWrote, third: endQuoted, fourth: endCount)))
	runEnded.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(runPlain.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(endAt, second: endWrote, third: endQuoted, fourth: endCount)))

	// Not before a quote: every backslash stands for itself, and the byte that ended the run is scanned
	// again from the top (it may be a separator, a NUL or an ordinary byte — none of this block's business).
	let pAt = ids.mint()
	let pWrote = ids.mint()
	let pQuoted = ids.mint()
	let pCount = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(runPlain, values: quadArgsOf(pAt, second: pWrote, third: pQuoted, fourth: pCount))
	let noLiteral = emitConst(module, block: runPlain, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(runPlain, op: StdOp.branch(emitRun.id))
	runPlain.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(emitRun.id, argIds: quintArgsOf(pWrote, second: pQuoted, third: pCount, fourth: pAt, fifth: noLiteral)))

	// Before a quote: HALF the backslashes survive, and the run's PARITY decides what the quote itself
	// becomes — even leaves it to toggle in the main scan, odd turns it into a literal this run swallows.
	let bqAt = ids.mint()
	let bqWrote = ids.mint()
	let bqQuoted = ids.mint()
	let bqCount = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(runBeforeQuote, values: quadArgsOf(bqAt, second: bqWrote, third: bqQuoted, fourth: bqCount))
	let oneBit = emitConst(module, block: runBeforeQuote, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let half = emitBin(module, block: runBeforeQuote, a: bqCount, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let parity = emitBin(module, block: runBeforeQuote, a: bqCount, b: oneBit, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	// An ODD run consumes the quote (`resume` steps past it) AND emits one; an EVEN run does neither, so the
	// parity IS both numbers and there is no second test.
	let resumeAt = emitBin(module, block: runBeforeQuote, a: bqAt, b: parity, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(runBeforeQuote, op: StdOp.branch(emitRun.id))
	runBeforeQuote.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(emitRun.id, argIds: quintArgsOf(bqWrote, second: bqQuoted, third: half, fourth: resumeAt, fifth: parity)))

	// The ONE emit loop the three exits share: write `remaining` backslashes, then the tail.
	let eWrote = ids.mint()
	let eQuoted = ids.mint()
	let eRemaining = ids.mint()
	let eResume = ids.mint()
	let eLiteral = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(emitRun, values: quintArgsOf(eWrote, second: eQuoted, third: eRemaining, fourth: eResume, fifth: eLiteral))
	let eZero = emitConst(module, block: emitRun, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let runDone = emitCmp(module, block: emitRun, a: eRemaining, b: eZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(emitRun, op: StdOp.condBranch(runDone, thenBlockId: runTail.id, elseBlockId: emitRunStep.id))
	emitRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(runTail.id, argIds: quadArgsOf(eWrote, second: eQuoted, third: eResume, fourth: eLiteral)))
	emitRun.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(emitRunStep.id, argIds: quintArgsOf(eWrote, second: eQuoted, third: eRemaining, fourth: eResume, fifth: eLiteral)))

	let sWrote = ids.mint()
	let sQuoted = ids.mint()
	let sRemaining = ids.mint()
	let sResume = ids.mint()
	let sLiteral = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(emitRunStep, values: quintArgsOf(sWrote, second: sQuoted, third: sRemaining, fourth: sResume, fifth: sLiteral))
	let sByte = emitConst(module, block: emitRunStep, value: BackslashByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: emitRunStep, base: dest, index: sWrote, value: sByte, ids: ids)
	let sOne = emitConst(module, block: emitRunStep, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let sNextWrote = emitBin(module, block: emitRunStep, a: sWrote, b: sOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let sLeft = emitBin(module, block: emitRunStep, a: sRemaining, b: sOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(emitRunStep, op: StdOp.branch(emitRun.id))
	emitRunStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(emitRun.id, argIds: quintArgsOf(sNextWrote, second: sQuoted, third: sLeft, fourth: sResume, fifth: sLiteral)))

	let tWrote = ids.mint()
	let tQuoted = ids.mint()
	let tResume = ids.mint()
	let tLiteral = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(runTail, values: quadArgsOf(tWrote, second: tQuoted, third: tResume, fourth: tLiteral))
	let tZero = emitConst(module, block: runTail, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let wantsQuote = emitCmp(module, block: runTail, a: tLiteral, b: tZero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(runTail, op: StdOp.condBranch(wantsQuote, thenBlockId: runTailQuote.id, elseBlockId: scan.id))
	runTail.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(runTailQuote.id, argIds: threeArgs(tWrote, second: tQuoted, third: tResume)))
	runTail.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: threeArgs(tResume, second: tWrote, third: tQuoted)))

	let tqWrote = ids.mint()
	let tqQuoted = ids.mint()
	let tqResume = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(runTailQuote, values: threeArgs(tqWrote, second: tqQuoted, third: tqResume))
	let tqByte = emitConst(module, block: runTailQuote, value: QuoteByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: runTailQuote, base: dest, index: tqWrote, value: tqByte, ids: ids)
	let tqOne = emitConst(module, block: runTailQuote, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let tqNextWrote = emitBin(module, block: runTailQuote, a: tqWrote, b: tqOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(runTailQuote, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))
	runTailQuote.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: threeArgs(tqResume, second: tqNextWrote, third: tqQuoted)))

	// --- the argument ends: NUL-terminate it and answer where the scan stopped ---
	let endPos = ids.mint()
	let endWrite = ids.mint()
	terminate.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(endPos, argType: StdType.i64))
	terminate.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(endWrite, argType: StdType.i64))
	let terminator = emitConst(module, block: terminate, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: terminate, base: dest, index: endWrite, value: terminator, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(terminate, op: StdOp.ret(endPos))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCommandLineScanOne'

// ⭐⭐ **A BLOCK'S CARRIED VALUES, DECLARED FROM THE SAME CONSTRUCTOR ITS EDGES SUPPLY THEM WITH.** Every
// loop here is a phi over i64s, and the hazard is not forgetting to push one — it is pushing them in a
// DIFFERENT ORDER from the `BranchEdge` that feeds them, which is a silent transposition of two live values
// (a write cursor read as a quoting flag) with no diagnostic anywhere.
//
// ⇒ So a block's parameter list and its predecessors' argument lists are built by the SAME
// `threeArgs`/`quadArgsOf`/`quintArgsOf` call, and this turns one such list into block args. One shape, two
// uses, no second ordering to keep in step. It replaces four hand-written per-arity pushers, two of which
// had already grown misleading parameter names by being reused at a different arity.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED for `ProcessRuntime` (W5), whose grow-and-retry loop carries a (buffer, capacity) pair across
// exactly this kind of merge. Exported rather than re-declared, because the whole point of the paragraph
// above is that ONE constructor builds both a block's parameter list and its predecessors' argument lists —
// a second copy in another file would immediately be a second ordering to keep in step.
export function pushI64BlockArgs(block IrBlock, values ValueIdArray)
	for value in values 'each'
		block.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(value, argType: StdType.i64))
	end 'each'
end 'pushI64BlockArgs'

// A four- and a five-value `BranchEdge` argument list. `MmRuntime` stops at three, and these two exist for
// the same reason those do: a literal `ValueIdArray.create()` plus four `push`es at each of the nine sites
// below is four chances to transpose two of them.
function quadArgsOf(first ValueId, second ValueId, third ValueId, fourth ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	var args = threeArgs(first, second: second, third: third)
	args.push(fourth)
	return args
end 'quadArgsOf'

function quintArgsOf(first ValueId, second ValueId, third ValueId, fourth ValueId, fifth ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	var args = quadArgsOf(first, second: second, third: third, fourth: fourth)
	args.push(fifth)
	return args
end 'quintArgsOf'

// One byte of the separator test, and the reason it is a function: the leading skip asks it twice (space,
// then tab) and would otherwise carry two copies of "load the byte, compare, branch". `miss` is where a
// non-match goes — the next test for the first call, the argument start for the second.
function emitSeparatorTest(module StdModule, block IrBlock, cmd ValueId, at ValueId, byte ParsedInt, hit IrBlock, miss IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let loaded = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: cmd, index: at, ids: ids)
	let sep = emitConst(module, block: block, value: byte, ids: ids)
	let matches = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: loaded, b: sep, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(matches, thenBlockId: hit.id, elseBlockId: miss.id))
end 'emitSeparatorTest'

// The IN-SCAN separator test — `emitSeparatorTest`'s twin for the loop that is already copying an argument,
// where a hit ENDS the argument (two carried values) and a miss carries all three onward. Two functions
// rather than one parameterized over the edge shapes, because the two edges genuinely differ in ARITY and
// folding them would mean passing both argument lists in anyway.
function emitScanSeparatorExit(module StdModule, block IrBlock, cmd ValueId, byte ParsedInt, ends IrBlock, continues IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let at = ids.mint()
	let wrote = ids.mint()
	let quoted = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(block, values: threeArgs(at, second: wrote, third: quoted))
	let loaded = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: cmd, index: at, ids: ids)
	let sep = emitConst(module, block: block, value: byte, ids: ids)
	let matches = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: loaded, b: sep, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(matches, thenBlockId: ends.id, elseBlockId: continues.id))
	block.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(ends.id, argIds: twoArgs(at, second: wrote)))
	block.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(continues.id, argIds: threeArgs(at, second: wrote, third: quoted)))
end 'emitScanSeparatorExit'

// Step both cursors by the caller's deltas and go round again — the tail three of the copy blocks share,
// which differ only in how far the READ cursor moves (one byte for a copied byte, two for a `""` pair).
function emitResumeScan(module StdModule, block IrBlock, scan IrBlock, at ValueId, atDelta ParsedInt, wrote ValueId, wroteDelta ParsedInt, quoted ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let readStep = emitConst(module, block: block, value: atDelta, ids: ids)
	let nextAt = emitBin(module, block: block, a: at, b: readStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let writeStep = emitConst(module, block: block, value: wroteDelta, ids: ids)
	let nextWrote = emitBin(module, block: block, a: wrote, b: writeStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))
	block.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scan.id, argIds: threeArgs(nextAt, second: nextWrote, third: quoted)))
end 'emitResumeScan'

// ⭐ **`__cl_count() -> argc`** — walk the whole line and count what `__cl_scan_one` finds.
//
// It answers the RAW count, argv[0] included, exactly as the bootstrap's `maxon_command_line_count` does
// ("returns total argc (including argv[0])", `X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:2032`) — `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`
// loops `0 upto count` and takes them all, so index 0 is the program and the user's first argument is 1.
//
// ⚠ It allocates the same scratch `__cl_arg` does and throws the bytes away. That is deliberate: the
// alternative is a `dest == 0` "measure only" mode threaded through the scanner, which puts a branch on the
// hot byte-copy path of the OTHER caller to save one allocation on a call that happens once per program.
function buildCommandLineCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CommandLineCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clcloop")
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clcstep")
	var finish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clcdone")

	let cmd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osCommandLine(cmd))
	let length = ids.mint()
	var build = emitCStringLength(module, func: func, cur: entry, cstr: cmd, length: length, ids: ids)
	let scratch = emitCommandLineScratch(module, block: build, length: length, ids: ids)

	let pos = ids.mint()
	let seen = ids.mint()
	loop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	loop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(seen, argType: StdType.i64))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: build, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(build, op: StdOp.branch(loop.id))
	build.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loop.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	let next = emitScanOneCall(module, block: loop, cmd: cmd, pos: pos, dest: scratch, ids: ids)
	let found = emitFoundAnArgument(module, block: loop, next: next, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loop, op: StdOp.condBranch(found, thenBlockId: step.id, elseBlockId: finish.id))
	loop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(step.id, argIds: twoArgs(next, second: seen)))
	loop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(finish.id, argIds: argsOf(seen)))

	let stepPos = ids.mint()
	let stepSeen = ids.mint()
	step.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(stepPos, argType: StdType.i64))
	step.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(stepSeen, argType: StdType.i64))
	let one = emitConst(module, block: step, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let counted = emitBin(module, block: step, a: stepSeen, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(loop.id))
	step.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loop.id, argIds: twoArgs(stepPos, second: counted)))

	let total = ids.mint()
	finish.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(total, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitRawFree(module, block: finish, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finish, op: StdOp.ret(total))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCommandLineCount'

// ⭐ **`__cl_arg(index) -> arrRec`** — the argument at `index` as a FRESH owned `__ManagedMemory`.
//
// ⭐ **AN OUT-OF-RANGE INDEX ANSWERS THE EMPTY STRING, WHICH IS THE BOOTSTRAP'S ANSWER AND NOT A GUESS.**
// `maxon_command_line_arg` bounds-checks and returns a 1-byte NUL buffer (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:2090-2106`).
// It is reachable from ordinary source — `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`'s `args()` never asks out of range, but
// nothing stops a caller — and a wild read here would be an out-of-buffer load, so the guard is real. It is
// spelled by NUL-terminating the scratch at offset 0 and then joining the ordinary copy-out, rather than by
// a second `__managed_create` path: one producer of the answer, whatever the reason for it.
//
// ⚠ NON-throwing. There is no failure available to it — an index outside the range is answered, not
// refused — which matches the intrinsic the bootstrap declares (`RuntimeCallToManaged`, no throws clause).
function buildCommandLineArg(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CommandLineArgName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var loop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"claloop")
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clanext")
	var outOfRange = module.addBlock(func, label: b"claoob")
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clacopy")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let index = 0 as ValueId

	let cmd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osCommandLine(cmd))
	let length = ids.mint()
	var build = emitCStringLength(module, func: func, cur: entry, cstr: cmd, length: length, ids: ids)
	let scratch = emitCommandLineScratch(module, block: build, length: length, ids: ids)

	let pos = ids.mint()
	let seen = ids.mint()
	loop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	loop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(seen, argType: StdType.i64))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: build, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(build, op: StdOp.branch(loop.id))
	build.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loop.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	// The scan writes the argument into the scratch BEFORE we know whether it is the one asked for; the
	// wanted one is simply the last write, and every earlier one is overwritten in place. That is what makes
	// one scratch buffer enough for the whole walk.
	let next = emitScanOneCall(module, block: loop, cmd: cmd, pos: pos, dest: scratch, ids: ids)
	let found = emitFoundAnArgument(module, block: loop, next: next, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(loop, op: StdOp.condBranch(found, thenBlockId: advance.id, elseBlockId: outOfRange.id))
	loop.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(advance.id, argIds: twoArgs(next, second: seen)))

	let advPos = ids.mint()
	let advSeen = ids.mint()
	advance.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(advPos, argType: StdType.i64))
	advance.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(advSeen, argType: StdType.i64))
	let isWanted = emitCmp(module, block: advance, a: advSeen, b: index, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.condBranch(isWanted, thenBlockId: copyOut.id, elseBlockId: loop.id))
	let advOne = emitConst(module, block: advance, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let advNextSeen = emitBin(module, block: advance, a: advSeen, b: advOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loop.id, argIds: twoArgs(advPos, second: advNextSeen)))

	// Ran off the end: make the scratch the empty string and take the ordinary copy-out.
	let emptyAt = emitConst(module, block: outOfRange, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyByte = emitConst(module, block: outOfRange, value: NulByte, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: outOfRange, base: scratch, index: emptyAt, value: emptyByte, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(outOfRange, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: scratch, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: built, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCommandLineArg'

// The scratch buffer both walkers allocate: the whole command line plus a NUL. See `NulTerminatorSlackBytes` for
// why that is a sufficient bound and needs no second scan.
function emitCommandLineScratch(module StdModule, block IrBlock, length ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let slack = emitConst(module, block: block, value: NulTerminatorSlackBytes, ids: ids)
	let size = emitBin(module, block: block, a: length, b: slack, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitRawAlloc(module, block: block, byteCount: size, ids: ids)
end 'emitCommandLineScratch'

// One `__cl_scan_one` call. Both walkers make the identical one, and a transposed argument here would be a
// scanner reading the DESTINATION and writing the command line.
function emitScanOneCall(module StdModule, block IrBlock, cmd ValueId, pos ValueId, dest ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let next = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(next, callee: CommandLineScanOneName, args: threeArgs(cmd, second: pos, third: dest)))
	return next
end 'emitScanOneCall'

// Did the scan find an argument? Both walkers ask, and both must read `NoMoreArguments` the same way.
function emitFoundAnArgument(module StdModule, block IrBlock, next ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: next, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
end 'emitFoundAnArgument'

// ⭐⭐ **`__cl_count()` AND `__cl_arg(i)` OVER A PRE-SPLIT VECTOR (MAC7).** The two entry points and their
// contracts are the Windows half's exactly — the raw count with argv[0] included, and the empty string for
// an index outside it — and the BODIES have nothing in common with it, because there is nothing to scan.
//
// ⚠ **`__cl_scan_one` IS NOT BUILT ON THIS SHAPE AT ALL.** It is the CRT's quoting rules, and applying them
// to a vector the kernel has already split would re-interpret bytes the SHELL has already interpreted: an
// argument that genuinely contains a `"` would come back with it removed.
function buildCommandLineVectorCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CommandLineCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	let count = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: CommandLineArgcLabel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(count))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCommandLineVectorCount'

// ⭐ **THE BOUNDS CHECK IS TWO-SIDED, AND BOTH SIDES ARE REACHABLE FROM SOURCE.** `stdlib/CommandLine.maxon`
// never asks out of range, but nothing stops a caller — and here an out-of-range index would be a load from
// a wild address rather than the Windows half's walk simply running out. A NEGATIVE index is checked as well
// as a past-the-end one: the parameter is an ordinary `int`, so `args.get(-1)` reaches this.
function buildCommandLineVectorArg(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(CommandLineArgName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var upperBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clvupper")
	var fromVector = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clvload")
	var outOfRange = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clvoob")
	var copyOut = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clvcopy")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let index = 0 as ValueId

	// The cstring reaching `copyOut` is one of two — a slot of the vector, or the empty-string word — so it
	// rides a block arg, which is the only way a value crosses a merge at this tier.
	let cstr = ids.mint()
	copyOut.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(cstr, argType: StdType.i64))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let negative = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: index, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(negative, thenBlockId: outOfRange.id, elseBlockId: upperBound.id))

	let count = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: upperBound, label: CommandLineArgcLabel, ids: ids)
	let pastEnd = emitCmp(module, block: upperBound, a: index, b: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(upperBound, op: StdOp.condBranch(pastEnd, thenBlockId: outOfRange.id, elseBlockId: fromVector.id))

	let base = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: fromVector, label: CommandLineArgvLabel, ids: ids)
	let stride = emitConst(module, block: fromVector, value: ArgvPointerBytes, ids: ids)
	let offset = emitBin(module, block: fromVector, a: index, b: stride, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitBin(module, block: fromVector, a: base, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let found = emitLoadField(module, block: fromVector, record: slot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fromVector, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))
	fromVector.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyOut.id, argIds: argsOf(found)))

	let empty = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(outOfRange, op: StdOp.globalAddr(empty, label: CommandLineEmptyArgLabel))
	module.setTerminator(outOfRange, op: StdOp.branch(copyOut.id))
	outOfRange.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyOut.id, argIds: argsOf(empty)))

	let record = ids.mint()
	var built = emitCStringToManaged(module, func: func, cur: copyOut, cstr: cstr, record: record, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(built, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildCommandLineVectorArg'

// `argv` is an array of POINTERS, so an index scales by the width of one.
let ArgvPointerBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **THE INSTALL FORKS ON THE TARGET, AND THAT IS THIS FAMILY'S WHOLE DIVERGENCE FROM THE OTHERS.** Every
// other runtime installer builds ONE graph for every target; argv cannot, because the two platforms do not
// hand a process the same THING (see `CommandLineShape`). `installSchedRuntime` already takes a `target` for
// a smaller version of the same reason, so the seam exists.
export function installCommandLineRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage, target Target)
	if not usage.usesCommandLine 'noCommandLine'
		return
	end 'noCommandLine'

	match commandLineShape(target) 'shape'
		unsplitString then installCommandLineSplitter(module)
		argumentVector then installCommandLineVector(module)
	end 'shape'
end 'installCommandLineRuntime'

// The Windows half: the CRT scanner and the two walkers built on it.
function installCommandLineSplitter(module StdModule)
	buildCommandLineScanOne(module)
	buildCommandLineCount(module)
	buildCommandLineArg(module)
end 'installCommandLineSplitter'

// The SysV half: a `.data` load and an indexed one. There is no scanner, because there is nothing to scan.
function installCommandLineVector(module StdModule)
	buildCommandLineVectorCount(module)
	buildCommandLineVectorArg(module)
end 'installCommandLineVector'

// Every callee this file installs — what `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set `usesCommandLine`, and what
// `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows target. A PREFIX test rather than a
// three-way `or`: every entry point here is `__cl_`-named by construction, so a fourth is covered without
// being remembered here as well as declared above.
//
// ⚠ It names `__cl_scan_one` too, which no source can call. That is correct rather than sloppy: the scanner
// is only ever reached FROM the two intrinsics, so a program containing it contains them, and the E3104 the
// prefix triggers is raised at the SOURCE callee's span in every case — the gate reads names the parser
// emitted, and the parser never emits this one.
export function isCommandLineRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: CommandLineRuntimePrefix)
end 'isCommandLineRuntimeCallee'
